
This directory is intended for PlatformIO Test Runner and project tests.

Unit Testing is a software testing method by which individual units of
source code, sets of one or more MCU program modules together with associated
control data, usage procedures, and operating procedures, are tested to
determine whether they are fit for use. Unit testing finds problems early
in the development cycle.

More information about PlatformIO Unit Testing:
- https://docs.platformio.org/en/latest/advanced/unit-testing/index.html

Lamp web regression tests (Node.js, no additional packages):
  node --test test\web_lamps.test.cjs test\web_motion.test.cjs test\web_buttons.test.cjs test\web_settings.test.cjs

These cover JavaScript syntax, command payloads, API error propagation,
the distinction between sent commands and confirmed lamp state, and the
shipped lamp configuration. They do not execute the ESP32 HTTP client.
Settings tests cover per-element partial saves, fresh sibling preservation,
concurrent edit/removal conflicts, source identity changes, duplicates, limits,
and isolation of local BWM and general settings.

Firmware and LittleFS image:
  pio run
  pio run --target buildfs

Browser smoke checks with a simulated API:
- Verify three device lists with Create buttons and a fixed internal BWM entry.
- Create opens a draft detail page; Cancel/Back discard without any config POST.
- Save changes only the selected entry; general/local edits send only their fields.
- Verify failed saves keep the draft, and conflicts can be resolved by cancelling
  and reopening the entry. Status polling must not overwrite edits or save errors.
- Switch fixed/sun time windows; hidden controls must not block form validation.
- Add, edit, save, disable and remove a lamp; verify dynamic dashboard rows.
- Test buttons must be disabled until changes are saved.
- Verify per-lamp HTTP errors and successful commands without confirmed state.
- Verify mobile layout and global EIN/AUS payloads.
- Both pages use the shared dark theme. Settings lists hide routine status text;
  dashboard lamps show only disabled/pending/error states, BWM rows retain activity.
- Verify errors remain visible, full details remain in the element editor, and
  Systemdiagnose/Debug Log are collapsed initially but can still be opened.

On-device checks after deployment:
- Load a legacy configuration without lamps: five outputs must be migrated
  with the old automation channel selection, including the button guard.
- Reboot and confirm persistence; an explicitly empty lamp list stays empty.
- Confirm startup AUS precedes EIN, including motion already active at boot.
- Verify automation uses only enabled automation lamps; long push/global
  commands use all enabled lamps. Taster/BWM event endpoints stay unchanged.
- Make one lamp unreachable: other commands must still be processed, with
  errors visible in status and logs. Retry explicitly using EIN/AUS.
- Verify the newest queued command wins, without suppressing startup AUS.
- Remove, disable or change an enabled lamp: old OFF target is sent.
- Invalid/duplicate IDs, invalid URLs, more than 32 lamps, and bodies above
  64 KiB must be rejected without changing the saved configuration.

Motion sensor checks:
- The web tests cover event URLs, status diagnostics, default migration mapping,
  and script syntax. Firmware behavior still needs on-device verification.
- Legacy config without motionSensors migrates 103/104 with old channels,
  IPs and polling flag; espMotionEnabled defaults true. Reboot preserves it.
- Disable internal BWM: GPIO activity and node=esp must not count; LED stays off.
- Add two event-only sensors A and B. Send A EIN twice, B EIN, A AUS twice:
  active count must be 1 and no OFF countdown may run yet. B AUS starts delay.
  Send A EIN before expiry: countdown is cancelled, light stays on.
- Unknown/disabled IDs and missing/invalid state must return 400 without
  changing any sensor. Wrong legacy channel is ignored with 200.
- Disable/remove an active sensor via config: it must no longer count.
- With no motionSensors and internal disabled, active count remains zero.
- Verify per-sensor timeout, poll failure/recovery, and a newer HTTP event
  arriving during an older poll. Status includes last source and age.
- Try 33 sensors, duplicate/reserved IDs, duplicate legacy mappings,
  invalid polling host/channel and non-boolean enabled: reject atomically.

Button source checks:
- Web tests cover independent addition, the 32-source UI cap, source fields,
  activation/guard options and saving complete or explicitly empty lists.
- On device, verify migration of shelly102Ip, including an empty old IP,
  and persistence of added, edited, disabled and removed sources after reboot.
- POST invalid/duplicate IPs, malformed options and 33 sources: reject without
  changing the saved configuration.
- Send short/long events from each enabled IP: same existing button behavior.
  Disabled, removed and unlisted IPs must receive 403.
- Guarded sources receive 409 during the existing protected-lamp command window;
  independent unguarded sources still work. Old Shelly 102 URLs stay valid.
- The existing source=ui test bypass stays unchanged, including with an empty list.
